ukrainians continue to be confronted  with scenes of bodies littering streets  as they return to towns and villages  abandoned by russian forces  ukraine's leaders want russia to be held  accountable for what they call crimes  against humanity  the mayor of the ukrainian town of  motijn 50 kilometers west of kiev local  residents say was killed in an  execution-style slaying  her husband and son by her side  this is our mayor olga patrina her  husband igor vasilevich and son sasha  these are our local residents who were  kidnapped from the village on march 23rd  reports are also emerging of russian  soldiers using tactics associated with  criminal gangs  to hunt down ukrainian volunteers  in the village of severinka the body of  a 44 year old member of the ukrainian  territorial defense found in a sewage  maintenance shaft  his wife said he'd been kidnapped by  russian soldiers  in our village there were traitors who  showed them where they were but most of  them escaped as did my son  but he took another man as well  in bucha one woman found her husband's  body in a basement nearly two weeks  after reporting him missing  when i came down i recognized him by his  shoes his trousers his face was  mutilated his body was cold  they turned him over a little my  neighbor still has a picture of his face  he had been shot in the head mutilated  tortured  ukrainian officials are calling for  forensic investigations by the united  nations and the international court of  justice a global reckoning for what has  happened in this country what president  vladimir zielinski has described as a  genocide  hundreds of people killed tortured  executed civilians bodies on the streets  booby-trapped areas even the bodies of  the dead are booby-trapped widespread  aftermath of looting concentrated evils  visited our land the killers  executioners rapists marauders who call  themselves an army and who deserve only  death after what they've done  even in the safety of shelters in la viv  ukrainians carry their anger with them  if i could go to war i would go i swear  i would go i would strangle and kill  these filth with my hands hatred pure  hatred the nazis did not do things as  this scum does peaceful people are being  shot at from morning till night houses  are collapsing families fall apart  children die every day you hear a child  died a child died how many you can't  count it anymore how many civilians died  we saw it all with our own eyes whether  or not russians accused of war crimes  ever face consequences ukrainians who  have left homes and loved ones behind  say young and old will struggle  to cope with the trauma of what they've  seen for the rest of their lives  zayn basravi al-jazeera lev  ukraine's second largest city has been  hit by more russian shelling  seven people have been killed and  another 12 injured assad beg is in  kharkiv  this is a point of impact where the  russian missile hit  now  it is clearly a residential area now  what they fire out are these small  tiny pellets  these metal pellets and you can see the  marks all around they have broken the  windows  you can see  all along this wall  what the the damage that these do  you've got marks all done around this  wall you've got glass on the floor where  it's smashed now they have a radius of  around 100 to 150  meters and there's a playground there  the car wind car window here was smashed  even a van all the way back there has  been hit  and it has a radius of 100 to 155 meters  and we believe that one person was  killed at this location again in the  middle of a residential area there's two  playgrounds either side of me  it could have been  way worse  in this location two people were killed  there's still  blood on the floor that you can see  through that sand that the emergency  services have put over there's still  bread on the bench from those people  that were  sitting there  and  the the missile landed  right in the middle of this playground  well joining us to talk about the latest  human rights watch report on ukraine is  ida sawyer she's the crisis and conflict  director for human rights watch joining  us from washington d.c thanks very much  for speaking to us on al jazeera so i'm  just looking at your latest report  titled apparent war crimes in  russian-controlled areas where you talk  about summary executions and other grave  abuses  tell us what you've documented  thanks for having me so we at human  rights watch we've documented several  cases of russian military forces  committing apparent war crimes against  civilians in areas that were occupied by  the russian forces in cherniyev kharkiv  and the kiev regions of ukraine so this  includes one case in the village of  staria by kiev in cherney of region  north of kiev where russian soldiers  rounded up at least six men on february  27th and later executed them we spoke to  the mother of one of the men who was  nearby when her son and another man were  apprehended and she later saw the bodies  of her son and the other five men she  said there were gunshot wounds on their  heads and their hands were tied behind  their their backs  in another case on march 4th russian  forces in bucha just 30 kilometers  northwest of keith rounded up five men  and summarily executed one of them  a witness told us how soldiers forced  the five men to kneel on the side of the  road and then they shot one of the men  in the back of the head right we also  documented how in march 6 russian  soldiers in forzal threw a smoke grenade  into a basement and then shot a woman  and a 14 year old child as they emerged  tell us how you collated this  information and what sort of message  methods pardon me you used to gather  this information and to document this  so for this research we we interviewed  uh people who were witnesses and and  victims of these these violations uh  some by phone and some who were able to  interview in person after they had uh  escaped these these areas where the  violations took place  obviously accountability is so important  in in um during wars i mean what what  are you hoping that this  report will will actually achieve  so the the cases that we documented  including the summary executions as well  as a case of repeated rape are  violations of the laws of war which  clearly prohibit willful killing rape  and other sexual violence and anyone who  orders or deliberately commits such acts  or aids and abets them is responsible  for war crimes commanders of forces who  knew or had reason to know about such  crimes but did not attempt to stop them  or punish those responsible are also  commonly liable for war crimes as a  matter of command responsibility and as  you're saying all parties to the arms  conflict any armed conflict in fact are  obligated to abide by uh international  humanitarian law just tell us what those  responsibilities are under international  law and human rights law  so under the international humanitarian  law the laws of war  all parties to a conflict are prohibited  from willfully targeting civilians or  civilian targets um they're prohibited  from carrying out indiscriminate or  reckless uh or disproportionate attacks  that could target civilians or civilian  targets as well as these cases of woeful  killing rape sexual violence torture  inhumane treatment of captured  combatants and civilians in custody um  so these are all violations of the laws  of war and we hope that the ukrainian  authorities they've launched their own  investigations there's also the  international criminal court which has  launched an investigation into crimes  committed during this conflict and  we hope that they will carry out  credible impartial investigations  looking at potential violations by all  sides thank you so much ida sawyer for  speaking to us from washington d.c  thanks for having me
 

